  "query_preview": "..\n\nWestern periphery of high pressure centered over the eastern Great \nLakes will continue to support dry weather under mostly clear skies \nfor the remainder of the day. Return flow from the departing high \npressure will allow the nose of enhanced low-level theta-e air to \nlift into southern Michigan. Increasing moisture advection and weak \nconvergence aloft will provide a focus for elevated ascent, \nsupporting a chance for showers mainly around or south of I-94. \nTiming for this potential will be from the mid morning hours into \nthe early afternoon. A weak elongated shortwave will move through in \nthe afternoon providing an additional chance for a stray weak \nshower, followed by a cold front late tomorrow night into early \nSunday morning. Overall forcing looks to remain weak for afternoon \nand evening activity which will cap PoP chances aob 15%. So while\nmostly quiet, a light shower or stray activity cannot be ruled out.\n\nA secondary cold front will enter Sunday evening into early Monday \nmorning which will be the focal point for showers/storm activity \npending where better forcing aligns along the front. NBM holds \nchances (30%) across the Tri-Cities and Thumb with a subset of \nglobal models pushing this potential south across the southern half \nof the cwa. In either case, PoP values remain low with minimal qpf \npotential. High pressure quickly builds in on Monday supporting dry \nweather. More active conditions will build in Tuesday through the \nmidweek period. There is increasing convergence between models \nhighlighting a frontal boundary setting up across lower Michigan. \nLonger residence time of this front will provide multiple chances \nfor repeated rounds of showers and thunderstorms, pending the final \nposition of the boundary. Otherwise, temperatures hold around \nseasonal normal values today and through the weekend, with slightly \ncooler temperatures building in briefly by Monday after the passage \nof the cold front.",
